From 9d7852cde8a402b179468269b33d7528954c288d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:55:12 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 55/62] super-amd: another chunk for data a migration moved
 already, back up from the end

A volume whose data an AMD migration moved to AllocatedSpace + 0x800
gets another chunk the way the AMD driver would carry it out: as many
data disks, the data not at +0x5000 - RC_SetupTransformParams gives
0x20222 | 0x2000: backwards, the regions fixed, from the end
(RC_TransformUpdateNextRegionFromEnd), the data going back up to
+0x5000 (0x22222 in the LD made, 0x12222 in the one it starts from).
The start packet has ProtectStart = capacity - ProtectSize, the log of
each region ProtectStart = IOStart at its start (all above it moved),
"100 %" both LDs at 0.  md reshapes it backwards (reshape_direction,
new_offset = old + 0x4800: the Grow.c and sysfs.c patches earlier in
this series), held behind the log region by region (amd_reshape_up);
md goes on from the log's region end after assembly.

More disks and another chunk at once stay refused: AMD would move the
data in place, in units counted with the old chunk for both layouts
(RC_TransformDynamicProtectSize) - md can neither stop nor go on there;
one, then the other, is taken.  So is a volume whose members grew in
place since its data went down: no room above them.

Tested with the AMD Linux driver (rcraid 9.3.3) in a QEMU guest, a
RAID5 and a RAID10, both ways: its logs and packets are field for field
ours, it finishes ours stopped midway, mdadm finishes its own, the final
LD the same.  Not yet tried on hardware or with the AMD Windows driver.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
